**Theorem 8.3 (there are finitely many, and at least one, exceptional
strengths).** The exceptional set in $(\sqrt\ell,1)$ is nonempty and
has at most three members. Neither endpoint is exceptional in the
strong-regime parametrization.

Proof. Regard $q$ as the parameter on $[0,q_{\max}]$, put
$p=k+\alpha q$ and $\omega=ph_0^2+qb^2$, and consider the polynomial

$$
R(q)=\omega^2(1-p-q)+(2\omega-1)pq
     =\omega^2v^2-(1-\omega)^2t^2.
\tag{CA.57}
$$

It has degree at most three, since $p$ and $\omega$ are affine in $q$.
On the open interval both factors being compared are positive. Factoring
the difference of their squares shows $R(q)=0$ if and only if $D_*=0$;
there is no extraneous root from squaring. At zero,

$$
D_*(0)=kh_0^2\sqrt\ell>0,\qquad
R(0)=(kh_0^2)^2\ell>0.
\tag{CA.58}
$$

At $q=q_{\max}$ one has $p=q=q_{\max}$, hence $t=q_{\max}$,
$v=1-q_{\max}$ and

$$
D_*(q_{\max})=\omega-q_{\max}
 =-q_{\max}d^2<0,
\tag{CA.59}
$$

using $h_0^2+b^2+d^2=1$. The continuous function $D_*$ has a zero in
the open interval. Since $R(0)>0$, $R$ is a nonzero polynomial and has
at most three distinct real roots. The strictly increasing strength
parameter in Lemma 7.2 sends these interior roots to between one and
three distinct strengths strictly between $\sqrt\ell$ and $1$.
At $s=1$ (CA.59) is nonzero, so its equality vector is the singleton.
This proof neither counts roots by enumeration nor asserts uniqueness
of the exceptional strength. $\square$

